WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.472
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.110
For me and the wildlife,
with the animals that you're

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:28.290
going to encounter so that you
know what the behaviors are,

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:33.030
learning how an animal behaves,
where it's likely to be found,

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:37.420
or how to find them is half
of what you need to do.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:40.410
The other thing is, I feel very
fortunate that I collaborate

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:42.980
with, usually, world
experts who have

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:44.808
an area of either
local knowledge,

00:00:44.808 --> 00:00:46.350
or knowledge about
something specific

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:50.010
and can give even more in-depth
understanding into the animal

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:52.440
behavior, the location,
the currents, or what

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.410
that I'm going to anticipate,
so that I can put myself

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:56.580
in the right places.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.610
But I think one of the
things that I look back

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:03.180
on my early life, I think it
was diving and taking pictures

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.530
because I loved it.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.990
And learning about the
animals and their behaviors

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:09.030
allowed me to find
the animals first.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:12.040
And not everything just
swims right in front of you,

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:13.710
and so obvious,
the little things

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:15.540
can be the most interesting.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:19.050
But learning to find something
subtle is really wonderful.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:21.210
And sometimes, if you can
just imagine walking down

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.010
the street, you
don't notice the way

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:24.610
the leaves blow the first time.

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:26.290
But if you walk it
over and over again,

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:29.610
all of a sudden the scent of the
road, the way the leaves move,

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:32.160
and those little rhythms
become much more interesting.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.960
And so having a little
more familiarity

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.930
lets you get more comfortable to
get those more intimate moments

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:40.170
that maybe only you see, or
that a few other people who

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:42.910
experience it see it and
photograph [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:44.040
a photograph.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:49.350
So to me, what a lot of
preparation allows for,

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.420
all while having
the understanding

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.580
that things happen
like this, and you

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.820
have to be ready to react, and
react in a moment's notice.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:00.540
Especially when I'm
photographing other people,

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:06.000
like in Cuba, I like to talk
to them a little bit before

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.330
I make photographs
of them, mostly

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:13.480
because I wasn't only
there just to photograph,

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.900
but I also wanted to
learn from their stories

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.060
and learn their experiences.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:22.470
And from that, it
helped inform how

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:24.528
I wanted to take
the image, where

00:02:24.528 --> 00:02:26.070
I would want to take
the image, if we

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:29.640
wanted to include some artifacts
of theirs and such like that.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:34.020
And a lot of the times, it was
also a collaboration with them

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:37.980
of how they wanted to be
presented in the photograph.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:42.660
Like one of the photographs,
the older couple on the steps,

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:44.850
that's [INAUDIBLE]
and [INAUDIBLE]..

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:47.070
When I was
photographing them, he's

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:51.820
like, I want you to snap the
picture when I'm bringing

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:52.910
the bottle up to my lips.

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:56.290
So he's like, I want you
to show me drinking this.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:59.570
And so a lot of times
it's a collaboration

00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:02.430
with the people of
kind of bringing

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:05.160
their own personalities and
experiences within the image

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:10.650
rather than either projecting
my view or my ideology,

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.890
I guess, on them, and
having a little bit more

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:16.136
of their voice within the image.

00:03:16.136 --> 00:03:19.210
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:22.370
Well, I'm trying to document
a lot of natural behavior,

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:26.370
so I would like to have
as little direct impact

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:27.330
on the animals.

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:30.450
In the underwater world,
what's really amazing is

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:32.233
they have to want to come close.

00:03:32.233 --> 00:03:33.900
And it's a big ocean
out there, and they

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:36.580
can swim away quite easily.

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.790
So in order to do
this, there has

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:42.120
to be some sort of little
momentary connection

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.440
between the animal and myself,
to be able to get to a range

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:50.580
where I can actually make a
picture that's reasonable.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:53.340
And so it's a wonderful
sort of feeling

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.800
to learn how to move your
body, to interact in a place

00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:01.320
to be a silent observer, but
also to be someone that can be

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:05.130
in a position to actually
capture something that's

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:08.410
occurring, a feeding behavior,
a mating kind of thing,

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:12.360
a curious whale that wants
to swim up to you that

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:17.380
is interested in
something, a shark that

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:20.279
is interested in sort
of moving around you.

00:04:20.279 --> 00:04:26.890
And so those moments are natural
and happen on their own way.

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:29.670
But getting in the right
place or positioning

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:31.710
oneself for those
to happen is really

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:33.600
an art form in and of
itself and something

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.570
I also spent a fair
amount of time working on,

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:38.923
is how to be in the right
place at the right time.

00:04:38.923 --> 00:04:40.590
And you always want
to be in two places,

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:41.920
and you're never
in the right place,

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:43.260
nor do I ever have
the right lens

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:44.290
and all that kind of stuff.

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:45.790
And so they're all
a million reasons

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:46.892
why things don't work out.

00:04:46.892 --> 00:04:48.600
But every now and
then, you have success,

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.530
and it feels really wonderful.

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:53.340
I'm definitely trying to
engage in the environments

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:55.860
that I'm photographing,
mostly because I want

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.420
to create those relationships.

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:01.040
But also having the
camera that I have,

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:05.390
walking around a lot of
these small towns in Cuba,

00:05:05.390 --> 00:05:10.070
a lot of times we would
hear chatter of people.

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:13.510
Like I would be known
in that little town,

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:16.100
I'd be this person
walking around

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:19.310
with this big contraption thing.

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:22.730
Yeah, when I want to
photograph, I definitely

00:05:22.730 --> 00:05:26.780
want to connect as much
as I can with the people

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:29.090
and create a relationship.

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:32.492
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.560
It's really the power
of observation for me

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:38.330
that is what's so interesting.

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:39.650
And I really enjoy that moment.

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:42.110
And I enjoy it not just with
the camera to take a picture,

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:45.362
I enjoy the power of
actually learning to look

00:05:45.362 --> 00:05:46.820
and watching
something for a while.

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:49.970
And I find that to be one of
the most enjoyable experiences

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:52.700
to just sort of sit and look.

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:55.250
Even when I'm not
photographing, I

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:58.810
keep on observing, trying to
find the little details that

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:02.900
really make a scene and
make almost mental images

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:07.700
in my mind of
inspiration of possibly

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:09.616
making that image later.

00:06:09.616 --> 00:06:12.690
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:15.950
In terms of tips
for people, I think

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:19.770
one thing is to pick something
that is easily accessible.

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:23.570
So in my case, I like wildlife.

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:25.640
So if we were
thinking of animals,

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:27.230
I might pick animals
that are local.

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:29.000
If it's a robin that
has a nest right

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:32.390
in your yard, a squirrel,
a cat, something

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:33.680
that you're comfortable with.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:35.810
And think of what
do you want to say

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:38.480
about that animal or that plant?

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:40.550
Or what would you
like it to look like?

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:42.710
And what time of day are
you photographing this?

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:46.310
Instead of having things be
arbitrary, pause for a moment

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:48.470
and think, what do you
want the image to say?

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:50.780
What would you like to tell
someone about that plant?

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:54.350
Why do you like that flower that
stopped and took that picture?

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:56.060
And then begin to
think about, what

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:59.120
are the shape, the form,
the color, the time?

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:01.880
And what is it that you really
like about it that caused you

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:03.440
to pause in that way?

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:05.880
And I think, as you begin
to question yourself

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:08.300
of what you want
to say about it,

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:10.850
I would encourage you to
then take a few other images

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:13.250
and say, well, how
can I enhance what

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:14.870
it is I would like
to convey about it?

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:16.370
Do I want to be
close to this image?

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:18.010
Do I want to be far from it?

00:07:18.010 --> 00:07:19.400
Do I want to be looking down?

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:20.810
Do I want to be looking up?

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:23.690
And I think those
are the elements

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:24.860
that you begin to have.

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:27.068
And so instead of it just,
oh, I snapped this picture

00:07:27.068 --> 00:07:28.700
and I really liked
it, to notice them,

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:31.070
and to really begin to
sort of ask yourself

00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:32.390
what you want to say about it.

00:07:32.390 --> 00:07:37.850
With photography, think
about a topic or an emotion

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:40.370
or something that
you're deeply involved

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:44.390
with or passionate about, or
that you find yourself wanting

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:46.490
to express or talk about.

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:52.490
And then think of the ways
that you can showcase that

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:54.350
within photography.

00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:57.050
And I would just end
on one quick thing.

00:07:57.050 --> 00:07:58.400
Golfing is easy.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:00.060
Bike riding is easy.

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:02.720
But these people practice
over and over again.

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:05.900
And no one believes me, but
it's a skill to do this stuff,

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:08.880
and you have to practice
this over and over again.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:10.340
And I love doing
this photography,

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:12.960
and I'm sure Caterina
does as well.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:16.188
And so it's something you
do over and over again.

00:08:16.188 --> 00:08:18.230
And so it's not just that
I walk up to something,

00:08:18.230 --> 00:08:20.660
and I get one time lucky on
this, although maybe that does

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:21.380
happen.

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:24.230
These are skills that I'm
working on constantly,

00:08:24.230 --> 00:08:26.432
on getting myself ready
so the reflex is boom,

00:08:26.432 --> 00:08:28.640
I'm right here with the
camera, and I'm ready for it.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:31.100
Boom, my lights are
right where I want them.

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:31.700
I'm thinking.

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:33.020
I'm very aware of these things.

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:35.150
And I think that that
practice is really

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:38.059
critically important in
the art of photography

00:08:38.059 --> 00:08:40.220
and storytelling, at
whatever subject you're

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:41.669
interested in photographing.

00:08:41.669 --> 00:08:44.270
So my number one tip is find
the subject that you like,

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:47.900
photograph it, and think about
what you want to say about it.

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:51.250
[MUSIC PLAYING]

